<%# locals: login:

    What watching a repository actually does, and whose account GitHub will
    record it under. Shared by the disclosure beside the Watch button and the
    notice on /subscriptions, because this is the one thing on either screen
    someone could later say they were not told — and two copies of it are two
    copies to drift.

    It states the consequence rather than the mechanism. "Prism acts as you"
    is not a fact anyone can act on; "GitHub records the edit as coming from
    your account" is, because it says what a teammate reading the pull
    request's history will see. The last paragraph is what makes it safe to
    try: the block can be deleted and Prism takes the hint. %>
<p>
  Prism edits the pull request's description to add the link. GitHub records that edit as
  coming from your account, <span class="font-medium text-brand">@<%= login %></span> —
  the same as if you had edited it yourself, and that is what the pull request's history
  will show.
</p>
<p class="mt-2">
  Only pull requests that change Markdown are touched. The link sits inside HTML comment
  markers, nothing outside them is ever changed, and deleting the block by hand tells
  Prism to leave that pull request alone.
</p>
<p class="mt-2">
  Watching needs admin access on GitHub, because it registers a webhook on the repository.
</p>
